<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Korps Marechaussee te voet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Korps Marechaussee te voet was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial counter-insurgency force formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in 1890. Its small, mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of Indonesian soldiers under Dutch officers, armed with carbines and the klewang sword, pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> through jungle and hills. Used by General van Heutsz to break Acehnese resistance, it became notorious for harsh reprisals, above all in the Gayo and Alas campaign of 1904.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korps-marechaussee-te-voet.jpg" alt="Korps Marechaussee te voet" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Hoynck van Papendrecht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marechaussees_O.I._leger._1896.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Korps Marechaussee te voet, also called the Korps Marechaussee van Atjeh en Onderhoorigheden, was founded on 2 April 1890 as a gendarmerie unit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army">Royal Netherlands East Indies Army</a>, the KNIL. Its first task was to secure and guard the fortifications of the concentrated line in Groot-Atjèh during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a>. It later turned to counter-insurgency and was organised in divisions, each made up of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>. Its men were native Indonesians and Africans under European officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1898 Van Heutsz became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and, with his lieutenant Hendrikus Colijn, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> prime minister, conquered most of the region. Following the advice of Hurgronje, they won over cooperative uleebelang, secular chiefs, to cut the resistance off from its rural base, and combined lightly armed Marechaussee units with scorched-earth tactics. Colonel Gotfried Coenraad Ernst van Daalen, charged with breaking the remaining resistance, destroyed several villages and killed at least 2,900 Acehnese, among them 1,150 women and children, for the loss of 26 Dutch soldiers. Photographs from the expedition into the Gayo and Alas regions in 1904 show the killing of large groups of civilians at the Alas village of Koeto Reh.</p><p>The main secular resistance leaders, Sultan Muhammad Daud among them, capitulated in 1903, and by 1904 most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> was under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control. Estimates of Acehnese dead in the war range from 50,000 to 60,000, and about 10,000 Acehnese fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. Guerrilla resistance led by the ulema continued in the highlands until 1942, and Gayo was still a centre of resistance in 1914.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Marechaussee served as an elite corps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army">KNIL</a>, specialised in surprise offensive tactics and long patrols deep into the jungle. Each soldier was lightly armed with a carbine version of the Dutch Mannlicher rifle, weighing three and a half kilos and 95 centimetres long, and a klewang, a sword, so that the units could move quickly through the forest. Its commander was paid like his peers in the KNIL infantry and its officers like those of the engineer corps. The KNIL eventually adopted the corps' tactics as a whole, and in 1912 the carbine became the standard weapon of all its native soldiers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Korps Marechaussee te voet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the advice of the Islamic scholar Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje, they eventually allied with the uleebalang against the ulama, and from 1898 General J.B. van Heutsz pursued the guerrillas with mobile units of the <b>Marechaussee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The longest conflict was the Aceh War, begun in 1873, which turned into a guerrilla struggle; it was largely ended by mobile units of the <b>Marechaussee</b> under J. B. van Heutsz, with advice from the scholar Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje, by about 1904, though resistance continued for years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li></ul></section>
